The way I would implement it is as follows: 


; --------------------------------------------------
ENABLEAUTOBANKING directive

enables automatic banking and packed far addresses

ENABLEAUTOBANKING <flag>

flag = true or false, true means we want automatic banking and packed far addresses, false means current behaviour



; --------------------------------------------------
DEFINEZONE directive

this just defines some rules giving it a zone number for later reference, we could potentially have 
multiple banked areas of RAM if banks are in different zones

DEFINEZONE <n>, <start>, <length>, <alignment>, <bankedflag>

n = 0 to 65535, default is 0 as per current meaning every bit of code is in the same zone
start = 0 to 65535, start address of zone
length = 0 to 65535, end address of zone
alignment = 1 to 4096, the alignment of code in that zone, default = 1
bankedflag = true or false, true means this zone is banked, false means it is not banked



; --------------------------------------------------
.align label suffix

this is a label suffix to denote which labels should be far addresses WHEN referenced from a different bank

normal label (CANNOT be referenced from another bank):

print_str:	; in a loop, call print_char
			ret

print_char:	<blah>
			ret

aligned label (CAN be referenced from another bank):

print_str.align:
			; in a loop, call print_char
			ret

print_char:	<blah>
			ret


; --------------------------------------------------
BANK / ENDBANK directives

<zone> = 0 to 65535, default is 0 as per current meaning every bit of code is in the same zone

note: a bank is not a zone, we are not specifying the content of a bank but rather the rules to follow 
when banking (which is automatic - see behaviour below).

i.e.

BANK <zone>
; code between BANK and ENDBANK adheres to specified zone parameters
ENDBANK


; --------------------------------------------------
Assembling behaviour:

As code assembles, you need to track banks for each zone - the start address and memory left.  Each zone may have only main z80 
memory, or it may have banked memory.  Just treat them the same.

Only labels suffixed with .align are byte aligned *IF* referenced from another bank / zone - other labels are as per normal.  
This allows a far call to a group of related routines and subroutines and only have a far call to the routine, the subroutines 
will be in the same bank and don't need to be far.

During assembly it tries to fit the block (current BANK / ENDBANK) in the current bank length remaining.
If the code fits, then it goes in the current bank, and we reduce the memory left for that bank.
If the code doesn't fit, then it goes in the next bank, the previous bank may have left-over.
If it is too big for the specified zone length, then we get a warning and assembly ends.


At the end of assembly, we get a list of bank usage and left-overs for each zone - ideally in a txt file.
Filenames below are just with a 16bit counter, but... whever naming scheme is ok... below is compatible with 8 letter filenames.

e.g. 

ZONE 0: #0040 - #3fff							; this is just the zone start and end
BANK 0: out-0000.bin, #0040 - #2000, #2000		; this is what is used, note: #2000 is left over

ZONE 1: #4000 - #7fff							; this is just the zone start and end
BANK 0: out-0001.bin, #4000 - #7000, #fff		; this is what is used, note: #fff is left over
BANK 1: out-0002.bin, #4000 - #7fff				; this is what is used

ZONE 2: #8000 - #a000							; this is just the zone start and end
BANK 0: out-0003.bin, #8000 - #9000, #1000		; this is what is used, note: #1000 is left over


; --------------------------------------------------
Example code:

				ENABLEAUTOBANKING true			; turn on autobanking

				DEFINEZONE 0, #0040, #3fc0, 1, false
				DEFINEZONE 1, #4000, #4000, 256, true
				DEFINEZONE 2, #8000, #2000, 1, false

BANK 0
			; some code that hopefully will fit in ZONE 0, note we configured zone 0 to have no banks, CODE BLOCK A

mylabel1:	call mylabel2
			call mylabel7
			call mylabel8
			ret
ENDBANK

BANK 0
			; some more code that hopefully will fit in ZONE 0 along with what is already there, CODE BLOCK B

mylabel2:	call mylabel3		; note: this calls a byte aligned label
								; assembler will later change call mylabel3 to RST 6; dw mylabel3
								
			ret
ENDBANK

BANK 1
			; some code that will hopefully fit in a bank of ZONE 1, if it is too big for the zone, through a warning

mylabel3.align:					; note: this label is byte aligned, the programmer said it 'might be aligned'
								; the assembler saw that it actually needs to be aligned as it is called outside of the bank
								
			call mylabel4
			ret

mylabel4.align:					; note: this label is byte aligned, the programmer said it 'might be aligned'
								; the assembler saw that it *DOES NOT* need to be aligned, so it doesn't align it
								
			call mylabel6		; note: this calls a byte aligned label
								; assembler will later change call mylabel6 to RST 6; dw mylabel6

			ret

mylabel5:	ret
ENDBANK

BANK 1
			; some code that will hopefully fit in a bank of ZONE 1, if it is too big for the zone, through a warning
			; it might it in the same block as other BANK 1 blocks, but in this example it didn't and went to the next bank automatically

mylabel6.align:					; note: this label is byte aligned, the programmer said it 'might be aligned'
								; the assembler saw that it actually needs to be aligned as it is called outside of the bank
			ret
ENDBANK

BANK 2
			; some code that hopefully will fit in ZONE 2, note we configured zone 2 to have no banks, CODE BLOCK E

mylabel7:	; blah
			ret
ENDBANK

BANK 0
			; some more code that hopefully will fit in ZONE 0 along with what is already there, CODE BLOCK F

mylabel8:	; blah
			ret
ENDBANK

note: 

out-0000.bin would contain CODE BLOCKS A, B and F which has no far calls into it, it might make far calls out though
out-0001.bin would contain CODE BLOCKS C
out-0002.bin would contain CODE BLOCKS D
out-0003.bin would contain CODE BLOCKS E which has no far calls into it, it might make far calls out though

